WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.353
Security-relevant upload events should be logged intentionally.

2
00:00:04.353 --> 00:00:21.974
Accepted files, rejected files, validation failures, scanning results, quarantine transitions, processing failures, downloads, deletes, publication events, and administrative actions can all help operators understand what happened during an investigation.

3
00:00:21.974 --> 00:00:26.259
Correlation IDs or request identifiers connect related events.

4
00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:29.023
A single upload may pass through an ASP.

5
00:00:29.023 --> 00:00:38.904
NET Core request, temporary storage, a scan job, a metadata validation step, a thumbnail worker, a database state update, and a later download.

6
00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:45.400
Correlation lets the team follow that chain without recording sensitive file contents in logs.

7
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:50.859
Logs should capture useful metadata without becoming a second copy of the file.

8
00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:59.773
File contents, document text, private descriptions, personal data, and sensitive titles should not be written into ordinary logs.

9
00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:12.004
Safer metadata may include internal identifiers, sanitized state transitions, size category, file category, user or tenant reference, and processing outcome according to policy.

10
00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:21.332
Privacy-aware logging matters because file names, document titles, user identifiers, and descriptions can reveal sensitive information.

11
00:01:21.332 --> 00:01:26.308
Even when the file content is not logged, metadata can still be private.

12
00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:32.181
Log access, retention, exports, and support bundles should be controlled accordingly.

13
00:01:32.181 --> 00:01:47.729
Monitoring should focus on defensive signals: unusual upload sizes, repeated validation failures, unexpected file categories, scanner timeouts, processing errors, download spikes, cleanup failures, and administrative actions.

14
00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:55.261
These signals support operations and security review without turning monitoring into an unsafe content store.

15
00:01:55.261 --> 00:02:06.387
Retention and cleanup policies should cover temporary files, quarantined files, rejected files, processed files, public derivatives, private originals, and logs.

16
00:02:06.387 --> 00:02:15.923
Cleanup jobs need monitoring too; stale temporary files and forgotten quarantine stores can become long-term privacy and storage problems.
